{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Module.LinearMap.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.cubic_neg_of_lt_root","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.cubic_neg_of_lt_root","kind":"theorem","line":194,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.lean#L194-L200","doc":"The converse direction, so the test is genuinely an iff and a reader\ncannot mistake it for one-way evidence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.lt_root_of_cubic_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.lt_root_of_cubic_neg","kind":"theorem","line":177,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.lean#L177-L192","doc":"**`q < θ` is EXACTLY the sign of `q³ - q - 1`, given that `θ` is the root.**\n\nThis is what lets the probe test \"is `q₀` below the smallest Pisot number\"\nwith a single exact rational comparison, and make no floating-point claim\nabout `θ = 1.324717957...` at all. The hypothesis is only that `θ` is a root\nof `x³ - x - 1` with `θ ≥ 1` — Siegel's theorem that this particular root is\nthe smallest Pisot number is CITED by the probe, not proved here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.cube_sub_self_strictMono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.cube_sub_self_strictMono","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.lean#L163-L175","doc":"**`x³ - x` is strictly increasing on `[1, ∞)`.**\n\nThe cubic `x³ - x - 1` has derivative `3x² - 1 > 0` there; this is the\norder-theoretic form of that, proved directly so no calculus is needed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.not_mem_integer_lattice_of_coord_not_integral","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.not_mem_integer_lattice_of_coord_not_integral","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.lean#L146-L159","doc":"**`B_3` is not in `ℤ⁶`, so an ILP over `ℤ⁶` excludes it rather than failing\nto select it.**\n\nStated generally: a point one of whose coordinates is not the image of an\ninteger is not in the integer lattice. Trivial, and worth having explicitly\nbecause it is a *different kind* of negative from §1 — §1 says a linear\nobjective cannot pick `B_3` out of the feasible set, this says `B_3` is not in\nthe feasible set. An agent reading only the first could reasonably think a\ncleverer objective might help. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.min_iff_blind_on_two_sided_direction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.min_iff_blind_on_two_sided_direction","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.lean#L130-L140","doc":"**Nothing but a functional vanishing on `d` can have `x` optimal**, which\nis the S4 conclusion stated as a dichotomy: either the objective is blind to\nthe direction, or `x` is not the optimum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.not_minimal_of_descent_direction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.not_minimal_of_descent_direction","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.lean#L109-L128","doc":"**The one-sided constructive form: a single better feasible point refutes\nminimality.**\n\nThis is what the probe exhibits at `[A6]` — it does not need a two-sided\nargument, only one feasible lattice point whose objective is strictly lower.\nStated separately because a constructive refutation is a different (and\ncheaper) obligation than the structural one above, and conflating them is how\na measurement gets reported as a theorem. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.linear_min_two_sided_kills_direction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.linear_min_two_sided_kills_direction","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/IntegralityDoesNotEscapeRelativeInterior.lean#L78-L107","doc":"**A linear functional minimised at a two-sided-reachable point vanishes on\nthat direction.**\n\nThis is the S4 face argument with the convexity removed, and removing it is the\nwhole point: `S` here is an ARBITRARY set, so the statement applies to a\npolyhedron and to a set of lattice points alike. What does the work is not that\n`S` is convex but that it contains a point on each side of `x` along `d`.\n\nConsequence for the ILP question: an integer program cannot escape S4 by\nnon-convexity, because non-convexity was never what the argument used. It can\nonly escape by failing to reach both sides — i.e. by the lattice being COARSE\nenough near `x` to have no feasible point on one side. A dense coefficient ring\ndoes not do that. ","signatureChunk":0}]}